ratory data  analysis and domain expertise to help  separate technical noise from biological  signal in our data we will also explore  how major research principles of  reproducibility requires to the  a deep understanding of methods we use  and incorporate a clear record of  settings data transformation steps and  outputs along the way the purpose of  statistical analysis is to learn more  about some population of interest like  humans in the whole world but very  rarely if ever do we have access to  every individual in the population so  typically we can sample a subset of the  population with a sample in hand we seek  to either summarize that data using  descriptive statistics or use the data  to make some prediction or statement  about that population using inferential  statistics these traditional methods of  statistical analysis were developed to  work with demographics astronomy and  biological data all of these domains  have been transformed by the emergence  of big data where each sample can be  described by thousands or even millions  of data points that can be generated  with increased speed due to the  automation of data collection processes  in many ways statistical analysis helps  develop an intuitive representation or  description of data properties helping  us find relationships between various  types of data we have about our sample  each data type can be visualized  focusing our attention on a trend that  is important once data sets grow  multivariate analysis methods can help  discover trend and big data and describe  the complex relationships between data  points in each sample in this session we  will discuss the relationship between  big data that is generated by automated  technologies called high-throughput  technologies and the methods of analysis  that are used to explore describe and  study the data leading us to the machine  learning paradigm  domain knowledge is critical for data  cleaning and processing before we  analyze data to inform