ey actually represent  meaningful patterns in the study at hand  as a result of these preparatory  sessions we will be ready to explore  datasets prepared for the program and  analyze them in depth for variability  and patterns we will take a few examples  and learn about the logic behind each  type of data for example we will see how  many projects use various datasets and  combine them together to explain  conditions and which elements are used  to define variables in each project we  will also learn about ways we can  validate our findings by relying on  large repositories in validation studies  in some cases we will discover that many  attempts and multiple comparisons are  essential before we find a true pattern  that is relevant to a question that we  might be willing to explore these  sessions will conclude week one of the  program and you will be equipped to  explore various types of omics data  using data wrangling techniques that  extract useful signals from big datasets  in week 2 of the program will focus on  multivariate analysis methods and  examples using machine learning and  artificial intelligence in session 6 we  will start a discussion of analysis  methods that can be grouped into  supervised and unsupervised methods we  will discuss how statistical analysis is  different from machine learning and what  exactly makes learning deep we'll have a  chance to see the different methods that  are commonly used to group data objects  by feature similarity and how innovative  mathematical thinking led to an  emergence of predictive algorithms that  can be trained to do prediction our next  session will be an overview of typical  methods and challenges in data mining  where unlabeled data is explored for  patterns  that might be significant for data  structure and organization for example  we will compare how different clustering  and dimensionality reduction techniques  can provide us with different types of  insights about the data and how we can  use the linkage criterion and distance  metrics define tune the algorithms we  apply to different types of data in  session 8 we will turn to supervised  methods of analysis and speak about key  concepts and examples where such data  analysis is used we will discuss how  training test and validation datasets  are prepared and different types of  classic methods for classification that  are used and the useful data  transformations that they can be  equipped with to deal with complex  features in noisy data and we will also  discuss feature selection that can help  us explore feature significance for  model stability and accuracy in session  9 we will discuss how models are  evaluated and tested to avoid  overfitting and misrepresentation using  tested validation methods for both  computer science and biological  standpoint we will see how accuracy of  the model is determined by key features  that algorithm picks to improve accuracy  and study feature significance and then  we will compare classification and  dimensionality reduction approaches to  understand how they can be used in a  complimentary mode we will also speak  about making these models more  transparent and explainable when we  present the results to our collaborators  finally we will speak about machine  learning and production and use a number  of examples that can help us see the  future of machine learning and AI  especially in biomedical applications we  will discuss classification and  application to biomarker discovery for  diagnostic purposes and an application  to target discovery and selection of  treatment and we will also speak about  the role big data plays in biomedical  research that regularly generates data  to develop a hypothesis that is then  analyzed and used for accuracy  improvement  and how this process is affecting  clinical care today and we will also  discuss other applications that are  already yielding significant results for  pharmaceutical and biotechnology  companies around the world to join the  program and get access to all of these  resources you will first have to  register on the science coach website  that is enriched with additional  coursework and many examples that you  will have access to during this program  the courses will be available for you to  complete and add to your personalized  profile where you can track your  progress and communicate with mentor or  program trainers your activity will be  assembled under your profile to show you  the overall progress with important  information then can help us discover  technical issues or lack of  understanding of a specific topic that  might be important for your project we  will also provide you with cloud access  to the team Island for computational  platform for processing of large-scale  next-generation sequencing data there  you will be able to run complex  bioinformatics pipelines without slowing  down your own computer and develop a  logical understanding of many of the  methods that we discuss throughout the  program after you have prepared the data  we will also explore the code behind  each step so that you can understand how  it works and modify it to fit your  project needs the practical coding  assignments will also help you  understand the R and Python code  elements that will be a part of any data  science project you end up working on in  your future these assignments are also  tracked and included under your profile  I am excited to be a part of this  program together with you I want to  highlight that the program materials and  many of the practical tools that we will  use were designed and tested by a whole  team of researchers and our team at Pine  I want to specifically mention the names  of dr. Alfred Tauber and dr. Lena  Brodsky that have put a significant  effort and resources to develop these  materials for your use welcome to data  science onyx 2020
 

